#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130029} :activity/id #uuid "c092012f-6280-5531-b39f-09c65d20ea5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050828 :topic/id #uuid "6961ffb7-2e37-5f54-ac39-0331f4517bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 1979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130377 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=g(x)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a45abf7904382a16daaa38ff617a9487b5c274cf65ef44f89e7feb0c32c496e0.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type I region**. Generally, a type I region is any region bounded by two vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ and two curves $y=g(x)$ and $y=f(x),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions. \n\nUsing set notation, a type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type I region, any vertical line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7282efbe2c9244c77ce6bbbeca047ee3ab29dd2fc90e0d2440c519328cb5973.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type I region since there is a vertical line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a2/a26c7588e297a8af35f1e709210596a94f3fb4bd84e8237dc1462f48b961630e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2658cc5e-d169-5ff8-871c-4ccaab428eb7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b518252-fe1c-57d7-bc6f-6a00d3addac9" :step/math-academy-id 22589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128992 :question/id #uuid "9cbb7321-753d-5253-8b64-072e3cc227bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050647}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393193}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"} :step/id #uuid "1b6899de-a336-56a9-809a-31cc01aeebb8" :step/math-academy-id 22590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130378 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}x=f(y)}$ and ${\\color{blue}x=g(y)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf2a2a41d63f21b279215fd1aa8d7361eb638e831b970bb3de861673f67612e6.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type II region**. In general, a type II region is any region that is bounded by two horizontal lines $y=a$ and $y=b,$ and two curves $x=g(y)$ and $x=f(y),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nUsing set notation, a type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type II region, any horizontal line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56019e8cc2b1146b99fa6f6532356281fdb847c342c25c63f843ddee4f8cb197.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type II region since there is a horizontal line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a7c1ead1274250f7184b132eff58a3028ce648715dcbfa7c7046077fd4e680ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d089f833-c09a-5229-9be0-8cffa2510d60" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3855 :tutorial/title "Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "4858561d-380d-50d9-901d-e33ce219f2d6" :step/math-academy-id 22591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128993 :question/id #uuid "cce0a7b2-7b25-5da6-8aa2-cbe8a40cd522" :question/math-academy-id "e-6102"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393200}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "952b663b-02bc-55c3-b391-c854df6de6fb" :step/math-academy-id 22592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130379 :tutorial/content "In general, a given region can be type I, type II, both, or neither.\n\nFor example, let's consider the region in the coordinate plane bounded between the circle ${\\color{blue}x^2+y^2=2}$ and the lines ${\\color{red}x=-1},$ ${\\color{red}x=1},$ ${\\color{red}y=-1}$, and ${\\color{red}y=1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db43502c2b096c912ac38d297c3a06e7dcbf4945af6fbc7f5e365a40d11fcde8.png)\n\nThere is a vertical line (for instance, the $y$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type I region.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1dca3b3015da15f554d95dae7add65aeee0b83a0075ec5295716a3e86d71f5.png)\n\nSimilarly, there is a horizontal line (for instance, the $x$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type II region.\n\n![](images/82/82720f4b69fc3e63bbf54cb22d60299035faf91a6021f09d41e9f62dad571581.png)\n\nIn conclusion, this region is *neither* type I nor type II.\n\nLet's now look at an example that is *both* type I and type II." :tutorial/id #uuid "09369aae-8943-5475-9739-20e6619b16d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3856 :tutorial/title "Regions that are Neither Type I Nor Type II"} :step/id #uuid "eafe2c8b-e7b1-59e6-9468-2c731ee86e98" :step/math-academy-id 22593 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129175 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128994 :question/id #uuid "cd9ce5bf-1663-5110-8aaa-8f0f47d7d30a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6103"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403653}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"} :step/id #uuid "3b78b691-c694-59d1-b1ef-5ea8a77ad679" :step/math-academy-id 22594}] :activity/title "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129333}]}
